## The problem context solves

When data is needed by many components at different depths, passing it through every intermediate component as props — "prop drilling" — is tedious and noisy. Context lets a value skip the middle layers.

## The three parts

```jsx
const ThemeContext = createContext('light');

function App() {
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
      <Toolbar />
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}

function ThemedButton() {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext); // 'dark', no props threaded
  return <button className={theme}>Click</button>;
}
```

You create a context, a `Provider` supplies a value to a subtree, and any descendant reads it with `useContext`.

## What it is for

Genuinely cross-cutting data: theme, locale, the authenticated user, a feature-flag set — things many components need and that change rarely.

## What to watch

Context is not a state manager and not a performance tool. Every consumer re-renders whenever the provider's `value` changes, so do not put fast-changing state in one large context. Split contexts by concern, and memoise the `value` object so it does not get a fresh reference on every render.

